From 205fa31e2b5a4b00a98817245f5045b4b2de824b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 18:52:45 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA: group same-activation quantized matvecs into one launch

Laguna S decode issues several quantized matvecs over the SAME activation
vector. In every one of the 48 attention blocks, attn_q, attn_k and attn_v all
read the post-attn_norm hidden state; in every one of the 47 MoE blocks the
shared-expert gate and up projections both read the post-ffn_norm hidden state.
Each is its own kernel launch.

Per-launch nsys durations on GB10 (sm_121, Q8_0 weights, 25 decode steps) show
those launches do not reach the memory rate the large ones do:

  attn_q       9216 rows, 30.1 MB   139 us   216 GB/s
  attn_output  3072 rows, 30.1 MB   138 us   218 GB/s
  attn_k       1024 rows,  3.34 MB   21.0 us  159 GB/s
  attn_v       1024 rows,  3.34 MB   19.3 us  173 GB/s
  ffn_*_shexp  1024 rows,  3.34 MB   20-22 us 152-167 GB/s
  attn_gate      72 rows,  0.235 MB   6.3 us   37 GB/s

A grid that only spans a few MB never amortises its ramp. This patch merges up
to MMVQ_MAX_GROUP_SIZE such ops into one kernel whose grid concatenates their
row ranges. A block routes itself to its segment (weight pointer, dst pointer,
local row index - all integer work) and then performs exactly the per-row thread
geometry, k-loop and reduction sequence of the individual
mul_mat_vec_q<type, 1, false, false> launch it replaces, so every output value
is byte-identical by construction. The one shared q8_1 quantisation of the
activation is made before any member writes, which is what makes issuing a
member at the position of the group leader safe even when its destination
aliases the activation's own allocator block.

Group members are not always adjacent, so later members are issued at the
position of the first. Legality is checked against actual data ranges for every
skipped-over node; nodes already accepted into the same group are exempt,
because they are issued by the same launch and read only the shared q8_1 copy.
Candidates that stock would fuse into a mul_mat+glu are left alone, including
the allocation-dependent range check that decides it, so no existing fusion is
disturbed. Nothing about the ggml graph changes.

THE DECISIVE DETAIL - segment selection must not use a runtime index. Writing
the natural

    seg = ...; vx = args.vx[seg];

makes nvcc give the kernel an 80-byte stack frame: it materialises the whole
parameter struct in per-thread local memory so it can be indexed. That is ~10 kB
of local traffic per block, and it inverts the result. Measured end to end on
Laguna S, same binary, same harness:

    runtime index (STACK:80)     mode 1 -4.90%   mode 2 -3.80%
    unrolled const (STACK:0)     mode 1 +1.109%  mode 2 +1.868%

Every stock mul_mat_vec_q instantiation has STACK:0; the grouped one was the
only kernel in libggml-cuda with a stack frame. cuobjdump -res-usage is the
check.

Measurement protocol: the policy is re-readable at runtime
(GGML_CUDA_MMVQ_GROUP_FILE), so both arms run inside ONE server process on the
same pages. This box has a decode-rate state that is fixed for a process launch
and independent of the code under test (~23.4-24.1 vs ~25.5-25.6 tok/s), which
whole-process A/B suppresses but cannot cancel. 12 rotated, reference-bracketed
cycles, median of per-cycle ratios, npred=192:

  no-op floor (same binary, same arm)   0.99994   spread 0.99916..1.00583
  mode 1  group rows <= 2048 only       1.01109   spread 1.01066..1.01417
  mode 2  group any eligible member     1.01868   spread 1.01849..1.02141

Mode 2 is the default. That merging the large attn_q in with K and V helps
*further* is the point: this is not launch-count reduction - GB10 decode is 93%
kernel time and removing launches is worth ~0.6% here - it is that one larger
grid reaches a memory rate three smaller grids do not.

Correctness: greedy text plus the full top-5 logprob vector at every position
over 3 prompts x 48 tokens are byte-identical across all three modes on
Laguna-S-2.1-Q4_K_M. Grouping is inert at batch > 1 (the detector requires
src1->ne[1] == 1), so prompt processing and perplexity are untouched.

GGML_CUDA_MMVQ_GROUP=0 disables it; GGML_MMVQ_GROUP_STATS=1 logs a line
whenever the effective policy or the groups it forms change.
